<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine Bodin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine Bodin was King of Duklja from about 1081 to around 1101, son of Mihailo I. In 1072 Bulgarian rebels proclaimed him tsar as Peter III, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> defeated and captured him. As king he extended his rule over Raška and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and secured an archbishopric at Bar in 1089. After his death the realm declined in dynastic strife.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-bodin.jpg" alt="Constantine Bodin" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Constantine Bodin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Constantine_Bodin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Constantine Bodin was the son of Mihailo Vojislavljević, the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, who held the Byzantine title of protospatharios and ruled Duklja, the most powerful Serbian principality of the time. His mother was a niece of the Byzantine emperor Constantine IX Monomachos.</p><p>In the fall of 1072 Bulgarian nobles led by Georgi Voiteh, the exarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>, asked Mihailo for a son whom they could crown as their emperor in a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. Mihailo sent Bodin with 300 troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a>, where he was proclaimed emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and renamed Petar, and is therefore sometimes counted as Peter III. His forces took Niš, but he was defeated and captured at Pauni in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. Venetian merchants whom Mihailo paid freed him in 1078, and he returned to become co-ruler of his father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bodin succeeded Mihailo in 1081. By 1085 he and his brothers had put down a revolt by cousins in the župa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, and he then ruled unchallenged. In 1081 he answered the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>'s call for aid against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> but stood aside at the Battle of Dyrrhachion and withdrew when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> were beaten. His support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> in 1089 won his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Bar the rank of archbishop, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> gained ground only on the coast, while the inland parts remained under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>According to the Chronicle of the Priest of Duklja, written in the 14th century, he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, installing his relative Stephen as knez in Bosnia and his nephews Vukan and Marko as župans in Serbia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, once they had dealt with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, attacked him, defeated him and imprisoned him again. He got himself freed, but his reputation declined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>What happened in Duklja is not fully known, and there may have been a civil war during his captivity. Queen Jaquinta persecuted possible claimants to the throne, including his cousin Branislav and his family. In the 1090s the centre of Serbian state life moved to the mountains of Kopaonik, where his subject Vukan led the fight against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. Dynastic conflicts and a struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> pushed Bodin into the background, and he ended his reign without power or reputation. After his death Duklja was engulfed in dynastic strife.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constantine Bodin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Constantine Bodin</b> briefly ruled a wide realm, and under him Bar became an archbishopric in 1089.</blockquote><a href="/state/duklja">Duklja</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Duklja. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.07,19.51&amp;year=1091" aria-label="Shkodër on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5024 666 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Constantine Bodin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5474 936h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5474 936h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.07,19.51&amp;year=1091">What was here in 1091? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
